### Bug Report

# Mooncake Issue Report: `CleanupStaleHandles` removes keys without checking lease, causing `lookup` hit → `load` -704 race

## Summary

In Mooncake store (both the deployed `v0.3.11.post1` and the current `main` branch), `CleanupStaleHandles` / `ClearInvalidHandles` erase object metadata based solely on `has_invalid_mem_handle() && is_completed()`, **without checking whether the object's lease is still active**. Because `ExistKey` (lookup) and `GetReplicaList` (load) each only `GrantLease` and do **not** `inc_refcnt`, a key can be reported as a hit by `ExistKey` and then erased by `ClearInvalidHandles` before `GetReplicaList` runs, producing `OBJECT_NOT_FOUND (-704)` on a key that was just reported as present.

This is a time-of-check-to-time-of-use race between the background `ClientMonitorFunc` cleanup and the two-step (lookup → load) access pattern used by vLLM-Ascend's KV pool. It is **not** fixed on `main`.

## Environment

- Mooncake store: `v0.3.11.post1` (production) and `main` branch (verified via GitHub raw source)
- Workload: vLLM-Ascend KV transfer (MooncakeConnectorV1 + AscendStoreConnector), GLM-5.2-w8a8c8, 8–12 node clusters, TP16/DP2 prefill, TP4/DP8 decode
- Three independent clusters, each with its own mooncake master HA (etcd-backed leadership)

## Observed symptom in production

Across three production clusters over ~7 days (2026-10-01 to 2026-10-08), `batch_get_into_multi_buffers` returns `-704` (OBJECT_NOT_FOUND) for keys that `batch_is_exist` (ExistKey) had just reported as present:

| Cluster | Master | -704 count | -703 count | -200 count | Eviction (delta) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| xpod06 | .76 | 4412 | 0 | 16 | 4 cycles, ~47000 keys / ~615 GB each |
| xpod07 | .171 | 4892 | 2 | 0 | 1 cycle, 70519 keys / 922 GB |
| xpod19 | .75 | 319 | 0 | 0 | 4 cycles, ~47000 keys / ~615 GB each |

- `-704` accounts for **>99.6%** of all non-zero return codes from `get`.
- `Eviction: Success/Attempts` is **delta per Admin Metrics cycle (~10 s)**, not cumulative (verified: consecutive non-zero rows are `1/1` with `0/0` in between, not monotonically increasing).
- After a manual leader switchover (metadata cleared, new leader starts fresh), `-704` drops to near-zero and then **gradually accumulates again** as the cluster runs. This correlates with buffer invalidation (`has_invalid_mem_handle`) accumulating over time, not with eviction pressure.

## Root cause (source-level)

### 1. `ExistKey` and `GetReplicaList` only `GrantLease`, never `inc_refcnt`

`master_service.cpp`:

```cpp
// ExistKey (lookup)
auto MasterService::ExistKey(const std::string& key) ... {
    MetadataAccessorRO accessor(this, key);
    if (!accessor.Exists()) return false;
    const auto& metadata = accessor.Get();
    if (metadata.HasReplica(&Replica::fn_is_completed)) {
        metadata.GrantLease(default_kv_lease_ttl_, default_kv_soft_pin_ttl_);
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// GetReplicaList (load)
auto MasterService::GetReplicaList(const std::string& key) ... {
    MetadataAccessorRO accessor(this, key);
    if (!accessor.Exists()) return tl::make_unexpected(ErrorCode::OBJECT_NOT_FOUND); // -704
    const auto& metadata = accessor.Get();
    std::vector<Replica::Descriptor> replica_list;
    metadata.VisitReplicas(&Replica::fn_is_completed, [&replica_list](const Replica& replica) {
        replica_list.emplace_back(replica.get_descriptor());
    });
    if (replica_list.empty())
        return tl::make_unexpected(ErrorCode::REPLICA_IS_NOT_READY); // -703
    metadata.GrantLease(default_kv_lease_ttl_, default_kv_soft_pin_ttl_);  // "so it will not be removed when the client is reading it"
    ...
}
```

`GrantLease` only extends `lease_timeout`; it does **not** touch `refcnt`:

```cpp
void GrantLease(const uint64_t ttl, const uint64_t soft_ttl) const {
    SpinLocker locker(&lock);
    lease_timeout = std::max(lease_timeout, now + std::chrono::milliseconds(ttl));
    if (soft_pin_timeout) { soft_pin_timeout = std::max(*soft_pin_timeout, now + ...); }
}
```

### 2. `BatchEvict` (normal eviction) DOES respect lease — this is NOT the culprit

```cpp
// BatchEvict first pass skips objects whose lease is not expired
if (it->second.IsHardPinned() ||
    !it->second.IsLeaseExpired(now) ||      // <- lease guard
    it->second.IsSoftPinned(now) ||
    !can_evict_replicas(it->second)) {       // can_evict requires refcnt==0
    ++it; continue;
}
```

Because `ExistKey`/`GetReplicaList` both `GrantLease`, a key that was just looked up is **protected from `BatchEvict`** for the lease TTL. Eviction alone cannot explain lookup-hit → load-`-704`.

### 3. `CleanupStaleHandles` / `ClearInvalidHandles` do NOT check lease — this IS the culprit

```cpp
bool MasterService::CleanupStaleHandles(
    ObjectMetadata& metadata,
    const std::unordered_set<UUID, boost::hash<UUID>>& alive_clients) {
    metadata.EraseReplicas([&alive_clients](const Replica& replica) {
        return (replica.has_invalid_mem_handle() ||
                replica.has_invalid_nof_handle() ||
                replica.has_stale_local_disk_client(alive_clients)) &&
               replica.is_completed();
    });
    return !metadata.IsValid();
}

void MasterService::ClearInvalidHandles(...) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < kNumShards; i++) {
        MetadataShardAccessorRW shard(this, i);
        auto it = shard->metadata.begin();
        while (it != shard->metadata.end()) {
            if (CleanupStaleHandles(it->second, alive_clients)) {
                shard->processing_keys.erase(it->first);
                ...
                it = shard->metadata.erase(it);   // <- key removed, lease never consulted
            } else { ++it; }
        }
    }
}
```

`CleanupStaleHandles` removes replicas whose memory handle is invalid (i.e. the segment was unmounted / buffer allocator expired) **regardless of whether the object's lease is still active**. When all replicas are removed and `IsValid()` becomes false, the key is erased

### Name and Version

version: 11381 (836d57176), built with Clang 21.1.8 for Linux arm64. Release package built with the Snapdragon toolchain image arm64-linux v0.7 (Hexagon SDK 6.6.0.0, tools 19.0.07), htp-v75. htp_main_thread is unchanged on master.

### Operating systems

Linux

### GGML backends

Hexagon

### Hardware

Arduino VENTUNO Q: QCS8275 (Hexagon v75), 16 GB; Ubuntu 24.04, kernel 6.8.0-1084-qcom, qcom-fastrpc1 1.0.15+repack2.

### Models

NeoHorse-1-4B (qwen35, 4.21 B) from https://huggingface.co/TokenRhythm/NeoHorse-1-4B-GGUF (NeoHorse-1-4B-BF16.gguf, rev 3c5d58ca), quantized with this build: llama-quantize --pure NeoHorse-1-4B-BF16.gguf NeoHorse-1-4B-q4_0-pure.gguf Q4_0. Needs two HTP sessions (HTP0:0,HTP0:1) on the one NPU.

### Problem description & steps to reproduce

```
GGML_HEXAGON_DEVICES=HTP0:0,HTP0:1 llama-server -m NeoHorse-1-4B-q4_0-pure.gguf -c 32768 --device HTP0:0,HTP0:1 -ngl 99
```
**Problem.** At a random request generation stops for good: host threads wait in `fastrpc_wait_for_completion`; no kernel message, no abort. 14 hangs in 898 requests in our logs (mixed configurations). `GGML_HEXAGON_OPQUEUE=1` did not help.

**Host side** (`dspqueue_get_stat` via a shim): one session's queue holds 1 request never dequeued by the DSP; the other session is idle.

**DSP side** (FARF via `llama-server.farf`): see logs. The last line is `htp_main_thread`'s `FARF(ERROR)`; the thread then **leaves its loop** (any peek error other than `EWOULDBLOCK`/`EEXPIRED`), so nothing reads that queue again. `0x2f` = `AEE_ENOMORE`, from the dspqueue's internal signal wait, mid-decode.

**Proposed change**: keep the loop alive (rate-limited log, 1 ms sleep, retry; `htp_iface_stop` still ends it via `ctx->killed`):
```diff
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/main.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/main.c
@@ -1349,6 +1349,8 @@

     FARF(HIGH, "htp-main-thread: started");

+    unsigned n_err = 0;  // consecutive dspqueue_peek errors
+
     while (!atomic_load(&ctx->killed)) {
         uint32_t flags = 0;
         uint32_t num_buffers = 0;
@@ -1356,12 +1358,18 @@

         int err = dspqueue_peek(ctx->dsp_queue, &flags, &num_buffers, &message_length, 50000);
         if (err == 0) {
+            n_err = 0;
             process_ops(ctx);
         } else if (err == AEE_EWOULDBLOCK || err == AEE_EEXPIRED) {
             continue;
         } else {
-            FARF(ERROR, "dspqueue_peek failed: 0x%08x", (unsigned) err);
-            break;
+            // Do not leave the loop: nothing else reads this session's queue, so the host would wait forever.
+            // Seen on v75: dspqueue_peek returns AEE_ENOMORE (0x2f) from its internal signal wait in the middle of
+            // a decode. Retry after a short sleep; htp_iface_stop ends the loop through ctx->killed.
+            if (n_err++ < 8 || (n_err % 1000) == 0) {
+                FARF(ERROR, "dspqueue_peek failed: 0x%08x (%u in a row), retrying", (unsigned) err, n_err);
+            }
+            qurt_sleep(1000);
         }
     }
```
**Tested** on the same board (same toolchain; unpatched rebuild byte-identical to the release library): 240/240 requests in 6 servers, no hang; 8 events of the same signature, each logged `(1 in a row), retrying`, and the session kept serving. Clean SIGTERM shutdowns. Not exercised: repeated errors.

**Questions:** is retrying after `AEE_ENOMORE` safe and sufficient, or must the queue be re-imported? Why does the signal wait return `AEE_ENOMORE` with two sessions? Happy to open a PR.

**Reproduction notes:** -fit changes nothing here (log: "failed to fit params ... n_gpu_layers already set by user to 99, abort"), i.e. as with -fit off. Not tried with llama-completion: the hang needs many requests on one running llama-server (about 1 in 30; prompts 255-1,462 tokens, 64-300 generated). Not #24963: its fix #24954 is in this build, and this hang also occurs in decode.

